__d(function(e,a,t,i,n,s,r){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 History",textbook:"Our Pasts-I",chapter_number:3,chapter_title:"In the Earliest Cities",total_mcqs:20,mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Approximately how many years ago did the Harappan civilisation flourish?",options:["A) About 8000 years ago","B) About 4700 years ago","C) About 2000 years ago","D) About 12,000 years ago"],correct_answer:"B) About 4700 years ago",explanation:"The Harappan civilisation flourished about 4700 years ago \u2014 around 2700 BCE. This is after Mehrgarh (about 8000 years ago) and before later civilisations like the Mauryan Empire (about 2300 years ago). The Harappan civilisation overlapped with ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia in terms of time period."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Who discovered Mohenjo-daro?",options:["A) Sir John Marshall","B) Mortimer Wheeler","C) R.D. Banerji","D) Alexander Cunningham"],correct_answer:"C) R.D. Banerji",explanation:"R.D. Banerji discovered Mohenjo-daro in 1922. Harappa was also recognised as an ancient site around the same period. Sir John Marshall was the Director-General of the Archaeological Survey of India at the time and supervised the excavations. Mortimer Wheeler later excavated sites like Brahmagiri in Karnataka. Alexander Cunningham was an earlier archaeologist who identified Harappa in the 19th century."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Harappan cities were divided into which two main parts?",options:["A) Market area and religious area","B) Citadel and lower town","C) Residential area and farming area","D) Administrative area and port area"],correct_answer:"B) Citadel and lower town",explanation:"Harappan cities were divided into two distinct parts: the citadel \u2014 a raised platform containing large, important public buildings (possibly for rulers or priests); and the lower town \u2014 the residential area where ordinary people lived. This two-part structure is found in major Harappan cities like Harappa and Mohenjo-daro."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What shape were Harappan seals?",options:["A) Circular","B) Triangular","C) Square","D) Rectangular"],correct_answer:"C) Square",explanation:"Harappan seals were generally square in shape. They were made of steatite (soapstone) and engraved with animal figures and an undeciphered script. They were used to stamp bags of goods being traded. The square shape made them easy to use as a stamp \u2014 pressing one face onto soft clay or wax to make an impression."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"From which present-day state of India did the Harappans possibly obtain gold?",options:["A) Rajasthan","B) Gujarat","C) Karnataka","D) Maharashtra"],correct_answer:"C) Karnataka",explanation:"The chapter states that Harappans obtained gold possibly from present-day Karnataka. The Kolar Gold Fields in Karnataka are among India's oldest gold mining areas, which supports this connection. This means Karnataka was part of a major ancient trade network 4700 years ago. Copper came from Rajasthan, and precious stones from Gujarat."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What material was mainly used to build houses in Harappan cities?",options:["A) Stone slabs","B) Wood and bamboo","C) Uniform baked bricks","D) Mud bricks dried in the sun"],correct_answer:"C) Uniform baked bricks",explanation:"Harappan houses were built using uniform baked bricks \u2014 fired in kilns to make them hard and durable. The key word is 'uniform' \u2014 all Harappan cities used bricks of the same proportions, suggesting a standardised building code enforced by central authority. Sun-dried mud bricks were used in some other ancient cultures, but Harappans preferred the more durable baked variety."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which Harappan city is known for having a dockyard?",options:["A) Harappa","B) Mohenjo-daro","C) Kalibangan","D) Lothal"],correct_answer:"D) Lothal",explanation:"Lothal, located in Gujarat on the banks of a tributary of the Sabarmati river, is known for having a dockyard \u2014 a structure for loading, unloading, and repairing boats. This suggests Lothal was a major port city involved in maritime trade. It was also an important centre for making stone, shell, and metal objects."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Harappan seals were made of which stone?",options:["A) Granite","B) Marble","C) Steatite (soapstone)","D) Sandstone"],correct_answer:"C) Steatite (soapstone)",explanation:"Harappan seals were made of steatite, also known as soapstone \u2014 a soft, fine-grained stone that is relatively easy to carve. This made it ideal for engraving detailed animal figures and the Harappan script. After carving, the seals were fired to harden them and often given a white coating."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does 'Mohenjo-daro' mean in Sindhi?",options:["A) Land of the Indus","B) City of the seals","C) Mound of the Dead","D) Great city of the south"],correct_answer:"C) Mound of the Dead",explanation:"Mohenjo-daro means 'Mound of the Dead' in the Sindhi language. This name was given by local people in modern times \u2014 not by the original inhabitants. The name may reflect the large burial mound-like remains found at the site. The Harappans themselves did not leave us a name for their city in any deciphered text."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What happened to Harappan cities around 3900 years ago?",options:["A) They were conquered by Alexander the Great","B) They were destroyed by a flood from the Indus river","C) People stopped living in them \u2014 writing, seals, and weights disappeared","D) They were abandoned because of a volcanic eruption"],correct_answer:"C) People stopped living in them \u2014 writing, seals, and weights disappeared",explanation:"Around 3900 years ago, the Harappan cities declined \u2014 people stopped living in them, writing and seals disappeared, raw materials from distant places became rare, and many sites were abandoned. The exact reason is debated. Alexander the Great came much later (about 2300 years ago). The cause was not definitively a single flood or volcanic eruption."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What does the presence of inspection holes in Harappan drains tell us?",options:["A) Drains were used to collect rainwater for drinking","B) Workers could access and clean the drains through these holes, showing planned maintenance","C) Drains were used to imprison criminals","D) The holes were used to let light into the underground drains"],correct_answer:"B) Workers could access and clean the drains through these holes, showing planned maintenance",explanation:"Inspection holes in Harappan drains were specifically designed to allow workers to access the covered drains for cleaning and maintenance. This shows that the Harappans didn't just build the drainage system \u2014 they planned for its long-term maintenance. This level of civic planning is remarkable for a civilisation from 4700 years ago."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The Harappan script has NOT been deciphered. Which of the following is the main reason?",options:["A) The script was written in a language that no longer exists","B) There is no bilingual inscription to help decode it, the inscriptions are very short, and the language family is unknown","C) Archaeologists are not interested in deciphering it","D) The script was deliberately made impossible to read"],correct_answer:"B) There is no bilingual inscription to help decode it, the inscriptions are very short, and the language family is unknown",explanation:"The Harappan script remains undeciphered because: there is no equivalent of the Rosetta Stone (a bilingual text that helped decode Egyptian hieroglyphics); inscriptions appear only on seals and are very short (not enough text to identify patterns); and we don't know what language family the script belongs to. These three barriers have prevented successful decipherment despite many attempts."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What were Harappan seals primarily used for?",options:["A) As currency in trade transactions","B) As decorative ornaments worn by rulers","C) To stamp bags and packets of goods being traded","D) As religious amulets to ward off evil"],correct_answer:"C) To stamp bags and packets of goods being traded",explanation:"Harappan seals were used to stamp bags or packets of goods \u2014 like a brand name, quality mark, or sender's identity on a shipment. When you stamped your seal on a packet, it identified you as the sender and potentially guaranteed the quality of the contents. This is similar to how modern companies put their logo on products. The animal images may have been like company logos."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"From where did the Harappans obtain tin for making bronze?",options:["A) Rajasthan and Oman","B) Karnataka and Gujarat","C) Afghanistan and Iran","D) Nepal and Tibet"],correct_answer:"C) Afghanistan and Iran",explanation:"The chapter states that tin was obtained from Afghanistan and Iran. Copper came from Rajasthan and possibly Oman. Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin \u2014 so Harappan bronze workers needed both. The fact that tin had to come from Afghanistan and Iran shows how extensive the Harappan trade network was."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is faience, and what did Harappans use it for?",options:["A) A type of metal alloy used for making weapons","B) A glazed material made from powdered quartz, used for beads and bangles","C) A type of clay used for making pottery","D) A type of stone used for building the citadel"],correct_answer:"B) A glazed material made from powdered quartz, used for beads and bangles",explanation:"Faience is a special glazed material made by heating powdered quartz with colourants and water. The result is a hard, shiny, bead-like material. Harappans used faience to make beads, bangles, and decorative items. Faience beads from Harappan sites have been found as far away as Mesopotamia, showing they were traded across great distances."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What was the role of scribes in Harappan cities?",options:["A) They were soldiers who protected the city","B) They were farmers who supplied food to the city","C) They were literate people who prepared seals and possibly wrote on other materials","D) They were merchants who traded goods with distant lands"],correct_answer:"C) They were literate people who prepared seals and possibly wrote on other materials",explanation:"Scribes were trained, literate individuals who knew how to write the Harappan script. They helped prepare seals \u2014 engraving the animal motifs and script. They may also have written accounts, records, or messages on materials like cloth or wood that have not survived. In ancient societies, literacy was rare and scribes were essential for administration and record-keeping."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Hard",question:"Why does the NCERT say the possible reasons for Harappan decline 'cannot explain the end of ALL the cities'?",options:["A) Because historians have not studied enough sites","B) Because each proposed reason (floods, rivers drying, deforestation) only explains decline at some sites, not across the entire civilisation","C) Because the Harappan cities did not actually decline \u2014 they are still inhabited","D) Because the decline happened too recently to be studied"],correct_answer:"B) Because each proposed reason (floods, rivers drying, deforestation) only explains decline at some sites, not across the entire civilisation",explanation:"The Harappan civilisation covered a huge area \u2014 from Balochistan to Gujarat to western UP. Any single cause (river drying, flood, deforestation) might explain decline at specific sites but cannot account for simultaneous collapse across all sites in such a diverse geographic area. This is why the NCERT says no single reason is fully satisfactory \u2014 the decline was likely caused by multiple interacting factors."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Hard",question:"What does the discovery of Harappan beads and seals in Mesopotamia (modern Iraq) reveal about the ancient world?",options:["A) Harappan people conquered Mesopotamia","B) The ancient world was more interconnected through trade than commonly assumed \u2014 civilisations thousands of kilometres apart were in regular contact","C) Mesopotamian people were more advanced than Harappans","D) Harappan people migrated to Mesopotamia"],correct_answer:"B) The ancient world was more interconnected through trade than commonly assumed \u2014 civilisations thousands of kilometres apart were in regular contact",explanation:"Finding Harappan objects in Mesopotamia (and Mesopotamian texts mentioning 'Meluhha', believed to be the Harappan region) shows regular trade and contact between two of the world's earliest urban civilisations, separated by thousands of kilometres. This reveals that 4700 years ago, ancient people were actively trading, communicating, and exchanging goods across vast distances \u2014 a form of ancient globalisation."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Hard",question:"What does the uniformity of weights and measures across Harappan cities suggest about their society?",options:["A) All Harappan cities were ruled by the same single king","B) There was a standardised system of weights enforced across cities, suggesting a centralised commercial and administrative system","C) Harappan people were not good at mathematics","D) Weights were only used in religious rituals"],correct_answer:"B) There was a standardised system of weights enforced across cities, suggesting a centralised commercial and administrative system",explanation:"Standardised weights found across all Harappan cities (made of a specific stone, in specific ratios) suggest a centralised system for trade and taxation. Without standardisation, fair trade would be impossible \u2014 different cities could use different standards and cheat each other. The uniformity implies an authority that set and maintained these standards across the entire civilisation \u2014 remarkable for 4700 years ago."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Hard",question:"A student says: 'The Harappans were more advanced than other ancient civilisations because they had planned cities.' Is this statement accurate? How would you refine it?",options:["A) Yes, the statement is completely accurate \u2014 Harappan cities were the world's most advanced","B) The statement needs refinement: Harappan cities showed remarkable urban planning (drainage, grids, standardised materials) that was not found in many contemporary civilisations, but Egypt and Mesopotamia also had impressive achievements \u2014 different civilisations excelled in different areas","C) No, the statement is completely wrong \u2014 Harappan cities had no planning","D) The statement is accurate but only for the drainage system, not the streets or buildings"],correct_answer:"B) The statement needs refinement: Harappan cities showed remarkable urban planning (drainage, grids, standardised materials) that was not found in many contemporary civilisations, but Egypt and Mesopotamia also had impressive achievements \u2014 different civilisations excelled in different areas",explanation:"Harappan cities were indeed remarkable for their urban planning \u2014 their drainage system was more sophisticated than contemporary Egyptian and Mesopotamian cities. However, comparing entire civilisations as 'more' or 'less advanced' is an oversimplification. Egypt excelled in monumental architecture and record-keeping; Mesopotamia in law codes and writing. The Harappans excelled in urban planning and standardisation. Each civilisation had unique strengths suited to its environment and priorities."}]}},2729,[]);